{"name":"Mint Tea","hex":"#afeee1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mint tea","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An herb tea made by steeping leaves of plants of the mint family in hot water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mint_tea"}],"entries":[{"title":"Maghrebi mint tea","blocks":[{"text":"Maghrebi mint tea, also known as Tunisian mint tea, Algerian mint tea, or Moroccan mint tea is a North African preparation of gunpowder green tea with spearmint leaves and sugar."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Tunisian_Tea_with_pine_nuts.jpg/330px-Tunisian_Tea_with_pine_nuts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maghrebi_mint_tea","wikidata":"Q937101"},{"title":"Mint tea (herbal tea)","blocks":[{"text":"Mint tea is a herbal tea made by infusing mint leaves in hot water. Mint tea can be brewed with fresh leaves, or with dehydrated leaves that have been dried to a moisture level of less than 10%. Mint tea made with peppermint leaves is called peppermint tea, and mint tea made with spearmint is called spearmint tea. There also exist teas that infuse peppermint and spearmint leaves. In Korea, traditional mint tea called bakha-cha (박하차) is made with East Asian wild mint leaves. In India, traditional mint tea called pudina chai is made by steeping spearmint or peppermint in hot chai."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Peppermint_tea_2.jpg/330px-Peppermint_tea_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mint_tea_(herbal_tea)","wikidata":"Q1071940"},{"title":"Tea","blocks":[{"text":"Tea is an aromatic beverage prepared by infusion of hot or boiling fresh water with fresh or cured leaves of Camellia sinensis, an evergreen shrub native to East Asia that originated in the borderlands of southwestern China, northeast India and northern Myanmar. Tea has a stimulant effect in humans primarily due to its caffeine content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Longjing_tea_steeping_in_gaiwan.jpg/330px-Longjing_tea_steeping_in_gaiwan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tea","wikidata":"Q6097"},{"title":"Spring green","blocks":[{"text":"Spring green is a color that was traditionally considered to be on the yellow side of green, but in modern computer systems based on the RGB color model is halfway between cyan and green on the color wheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/%D0%97%D2%AF%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82.jpg/330px-%D0%97%D2%AF%D0%BC%D1%96%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_green","wikidata":"Q373787"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/TeaSeremonyWS.jpg/500px-TeaSeremonyWS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/TeaSeremonyWS.jpg/1280px-TeaSeremonyWS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/TeaSeremonyWS.jpg/500px-TeaSeremonyWS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Saharawi tea ceremony","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATeaSeremonyWS.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ThiMil_The1.jpg/330px-ThiMil_The1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/ThiMil_The1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/ThiMil_The1.jpg/330px-ThiMil_The